      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            Spanish nouns and adjectives change form to indicate number. \nNouns ending in a vowel typically add -s to form the plural, while nouns ending in a consonant add -es. \nAdjectives must agree in number with the nouns they modify, so if the noun becomes plural, so does the adjective. \nIndefinite and definite articles also have singular and plural forms (un/una → unos/unas; el/la → los/las). \nThese substitution drills practise converting singular sentences into plural and choosing between options (same/other, little/much) to reinforce number agreement and quantifier usage.\n"
    }
